# Licences

LuaDocs is licensed in two parts, because prose and example code are used
differently ([ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-dual-license-prose-and-code.md)).

## Prose — CC BY 4.0

Everything written on this site — entry descriptions, guides, gotchas, notes —
is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence.

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

You may share and adapt it, including commercially, provided you give
appropriate credit and indicate whether changes were made.

## Example code — CC0 1.0

Every runnable example — anything inside a `<RunnableExample>` block, and any
fenced Lua in an entry — is released into the public domain under CC0 1.0.

https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

Copy it into your own project without attribution, without asking, and without
obligation. Requiring credit for a two-line example would be friction against the
point of publishing it.

## The boundary

Prose is the sentences. Code is what you would paste into an editor. Where a
sentence quotes a fragment of code, the fragment is CC0 and the sentence is
CC BY.

## What this does not cover

**The Lua reference manual.** Entries are written from the official manual, and
each one names the passage it was written from. That manual is not ours:

    Copyright © 1994–2026 Lua.org, PUC-Rio.
    Freely available under the terms of the Lua license.
    https://www.lua.org/license.html

The Lua licence is the MIT licence. Its terms cover the manual as well as the
software, and any use of that material — by us or by anyone reusing this site —
carries them.

**Lua itself**, and the WebAssembly build that runs examples in the browser
([Wasmoon](https://github.com/ceifa/wasmoon)), are the work of their own authors
under their own licences.

## Contributions

Contributing prose to this repository means licensing it under CC BY 4.0, and
contributing example code means releasing it under CC0. Content licences are
close to irreversible once outside contributors have added work, which is why
this is settled before the invitation to contribute is made rather than after.
